h's crust at any moment, named from the Greek for unstable; first made in 1940 by firing alpha particles at bismuth in a cyclotron after years of false claims, and found in nature in 1943 in the decay chains of uranium and thorium; even its longest-lived form lasts only hours, and any visible speck would boil away in its own heat, so no one has ever seen a piece of it, only guessed it would be a dark, metallic-looking solid; the heaviest halogen, it seeks the thyroid like iodine and, living about seven hours, is being shaped into an alpha-ray medicine that strikes only cancer cells.\nAnatomy: one head, two arms and two legs, each joined at its natural joint; natural hands with five fingers.\n\n3. COSTUME — FANTASY\nCostume: a high-fantasy royal or warrior costume — plate pauldrons or a gorget, mail or scale over a long tabard or robe, a circlet or crown, a flowing cape; metal ornament and regalia built from the traits above, subtle magical glow along the patterns.\nSilhouette: angular, spiked dark plate armor and a long cape with torn edges — a sharp, menacing silhouette. Only the overall shape is fixed; design the details freely in the element's materials and palette. This silhouette overrides the overall shape of the costume above.\nColours: near-black metallic gunmetal, faint vanishing violet, pinpoint white sparks, cold halogen grey-blue.\nExposure (bold): her regalia opens to show skin — bare shoulders and arms between pauldron and bracer, a deep open neckline under the gorget with natural cleavage, a high slit in the tabard or robe showing the thigh; armor pieces stay on and symmetrical. Nothing slipped off. Intimate areas covered; no corset; keep the costume above, material and palette.\n\n4. SCENE\nWorld: a kingdom that never gathers: its whole people are single dark-violet sparks hidden one by one in rock cracks across the continent; only on a glittering plain of black crystal do they gather for a few moments into a citadel of dark metal that begins to boil away in its own heat before it is ever finished, its half-built spires already thinning into faint vapour.\nPose: standing confidently, her body turned slightly while her face looks toward the viewer.\nLight: natural daylight or the world's own magical glow.\nComposition: vertical 2:3 full-body framing with modest headroom and clear space below the boots — the whole figure from the top of her head or headpiece to both feet stays inside the frame, occupying about four-fifths of the image height, with the same camera distance in every world; her hands stay inside the frame. Camera level with her face with natural portrait perspective; her head level, chin neither raised nor tucked — no upward perspective on her face, no wide-angle distortion, no low angle, no close crop.\n\n5. CONSTRAINTS\nOne figure only; no text or numbers except her element's own chemical symbol (At) and atomic number (85) — and those only as part of the scene: engraved, embroidered or glowing on her costume, a banner, a sign or a machine, never as a floating label, corner badge, periodic-table tile, caption or overlay on the picture; no logo or watermark.\nAvoid: flat single-tone cel shading, photographic appearance, semi-realistic face, plastic 3D-render finish, corset, bustle, Victorian tailoring, modern material, machinery.",
