 bare shoulders or arms, a high slit or short hem); intimate areas covered.\n\nFace proportions: a short midface and a soft, gently rounded jawline with a small soft chin; any crown or headpiece sits above her hairline. Her eyes keep the shape and size described above.\n\nComposition: vertical 2:3 full-body framing with modest headroom and clear space below the boots — the whole figure from the top of her head or headpiece to both feet stays inside the frame, occupying about four-fifths of the image height, with the same camera distance in every world; her hands stay inside the frame. Camera level with her face with natural portrait perspective; her head level, chin neither raised nor tucked — no upward perspective on her face, no wide-angle distortion, no low angle, no close crop.\n\nIf you have already drawn a free design of her in this session, make this one clearly different — another face and hairstyle (the same hair colour), another costume shape with another signature idea, another pose and setting.\n\nDetailed design for this image: Choose silhouette 1, light leather ranger armor with a short cloak, lowered hood, bracers and boots. Signature construction: the short cloak consists of nested crescent-cut layers with open gaps, visibly breaking into ever smaller pointed sections toward the hem, suggesting half-life and dispersal. Near-black gunmetal leather with cold grey-blue lining and faint violet edges, pinpoint white clasps, deep V neckline and short split tunic, bare arms and upper thighs with intimate areas opaque. Adult woman with luminous fair beige skin, soft oval face, turquoise almond eyes, fine straight brows, small nose, rose lips and an alert slight smile. Short chin and short midface. Jet-black metallic hair with violet sheen and scattered tiny white pinpoints, a high long ponytail and airy side fringe. Stand front-facing with weight shifted onto one boot and both empty hands opened slightly beside her hips, all five fingers visible. Set her beside a narrow fissure in the sunlit black-crystal plain with isolated dark-violet sparks in its cracks; far away a citadel's upper spires dissolve. No weapon. One figure only, natural hands with five fingers; no text or numbers except her element's own chemical symbol (At) and atomic number (85) — and those only as part of the scene: engraved, embroidered or glowing on her costume, a banner, a sign or a machine, never as a floating label, corner badge, periodic-table tile, caption or overlay on the picture; no logo or watermark.",
    "steampunk1": "A full-body vertical 2:3 (1024×1536) character card image of an adult woman — the personification of the element Astatine (아스타틴) itself — the sovereign of the Astatine Nation. A female character who IS the element; her costume, ornaments and colors carry the element’s traits. Both hands are ordinary human hands and hold nothing.\n\n1. STYLE\nA Japanese anime key visual with a painterly finish: an anime-style character with an elegant adult anime face, large expressive eyes, a small nose and mouth, fine coloured linework and soft cel-to-painted shading with painted highlights in the hair and fabric. Behind her, a richly painted background with layered painterly colour, subtle visible brushwork, atmospheric perspective and cinematic depth. Luminous, rich colours; crisp focal details on the face and costume, softer painted edges toward the distance.\nFace proportions: a short midface and a soft, gently rounded jawline with a small soft chin; any crown or headpiece sits above her hairline. Her eyes keep the shape and size described below.\n\n2. CHARACTER\nAge: mid-twenties — a clear adult face; no facial lines.\nFace — her own: a sharp, striking, intense look; deep brown skin; softly tapered face narrowing to a small, soft chin; smaller, narrower eyes than usual anime proportion — long monolid eyes, outer eye corners slightly lifted; sharp angled brows; aquiline nose; thin long lips; near-black obsidian eyes; smoky dark eye makeup.\nBody and hair: lean and toned — a slim, firm waist with softly defined vertical abs and lightly sculpted arms and shoulders, a full bust build; big, wild spiky hair with choppy uneven bangs; jet-black metallic hair with a faint violet sheen, scattered with a few tiny white pinpoints that wink in and out.\nHair: her hair is worn exactly as described above — loose, long or cut as named; do not pin it up into a bun or tuck it under goggles or a hat unless the hair above is an updo.\nExpression: a faint wistful smile, soft eyes.\nHer element, carried in her design: astatine: the rarest naturally occurring element, less than a gram of it spread through the whole of the Earth's crust at any moment, named from the Greek for unstable; first made in 1940 by firing alpha particles at bismuth in a cyclotron after years of false claims, and found in nature in 1943 in the decay chains of uranium and thorium; even its longest-lived form lasts only hours, and any visible speck would boil away in its own heat, so no one has ever seen a piece of it, only guessed it would be a dark, metallic-looking solid; the heaviest halogen, it seeks the thyroid like iodine and, living about seven hours, is being shaped into an alpha-ray medicine that strikes only cancer cells.\nAnatomy: one head, two arms and two legs, each joined at its natural joint; natural hands with five fingers.\n\n3. COSTUME — STEAMPUNK\nCostume: late-Victorian industrial dress — a high-necked blouse with a waistcoat or a tailored tail coat, leather gloves, a work apron or utility belt with brass fittings, goggles hung at the neck or a small instrument as ornament; below, a short bustle skirt and laced boots; the traits above as colour accents. No crown unless the opening line says she wears one; steam, pipes and gauges in the scene.\nSilhouette: a clockwork gear rig worn on her back over a fitted vest, small brass fittings on arms and legs — the machinery makes the shape. Only the overall shape is fixed; design the details freely in the element's materials and palette. This silhouette overrides the overall shape of the costume above.\nColours: near-black metallic gunmetal, faint vanishing violet, pinpoint white sparks, cold halogen grey-blue.\nExposure (bold): her outfit opens to show skin — the neckline opened down to the sternum with natural cleavage, bare forearms, and bare legs above the boots or through a high slit where the silhouette allows; pieces stay symmetrical. Nothing slipped off. Intimate areas covered; no corset; keep the costume above, material and palette.\n\n4. SCENE\nWorld: around 1940, a radiation laboratory on a hillside above a bay: a giant cyclotron magnet under a round roof, a bismuth target plate fitted to the vacuum chamber, a bench of clicking counters, and a blackboard covered in crossed-out chalk marks from years of false claims for the missing element below iodine.\nPose: standing confidently, her body turned slightly while her face looks toward the viewer.\nLight: bright warm sunlight with soft sky light bounced from white clouds.\nComposition: vertical 2:3 full-body framing with modest headroom and clear space below the boots — the whole figure from the top of her head or headpiece to both feet stays inside the frame, occupying about four-fifths of the image height, with the same camera distance in every world; her hands stay inside the frame. Camera level with her face with natural portrait perspective; her head level, chin neither raised nor tucked — no upward perspective on her face, no wide-angle distortion, no low angle, no close crop.\n\n5. CONSTRAINTS\nOne figure only; no text or numbers except her element's own chemical symbol (At) and atomic number (85) — and those only as part of the scene: engraved, embroidered or glowing on her costume, a banner, a sign or a machine, never as a floating label, corner badge, periodic-table tile, caption or overlay on the picture; no logo or watermark.\nAvoid: flat single-tone cel shading, photographic appearance, semi-realistic face, plastic 3D-render finish, armor plates, cape, modern plastic, sci-fi.",
